program covenant_reporting

-- ============================================================
-- Covenant reporting: the loan's calendar as admission law
-- ============================================================
--
-- A company that borrows a large sum does not just owe the money
-- back. The loan agreement makes it keep promises while the debt is
-- outstanding - covenants: keep debt below so many times earnings,
-- keep interest covered, and so on. The lender cannot watch the
-- borrower's books directly, so the agreement builds a reporting
-- calendar instead. Time is cut into test periods. When a period
-- ends, the borrower measures its covenants over it and delivers a
-- compliance certificate - a signed statement that the promises
-- held - within a fixed number of days. Miss the deadline and the
-- lateness itself is a default, usually pricing up the loan for
-- every day the certificate is missing.
--
-- Everything here runs on civil dates - the dates a contract names,
-- like 2026-11-30 - not on clock time. And civil dates have their
-- own arithmetic. "Three months after 30 November" is not a number
-- of seconds; it is a walk through the calendar, and the calendar's
-- months are not the same length. This programme is the one that
-- brought that arithmetic into Morpholog: a span like span(P3M) -
-- three calendar months - can shift a date, and subtracting one
-- date from another counts the actual days between them. Both are
-- used only inside the rules. No rule here ever invents a stored
-- figure; whenever a figure like a day count is admitted, it is the
-- caller who states it and the record that refuses it unless it is
-- exactly right.

-- ------------------------------------------------------------
-- The claims: what this register is prepared to say
-- ------------------------------------------------------------

-- One facility (the loan), opened with the end date of its first
-- test period - the anchor the whole calendar grows from. One
-- facility has one anchor: the identifier determines the record.
predicate Facility(facility: Subject, first_period_ends: Date)
    unique by (facility)

-- One test period of one facility, named by its own identifier and
-- carrying the date it ends on. Periods are history: once admitted,
-- never rewritten - the schedule may only grow.
predicate TestPeriod(facility: Subject, period: Subject, ends_on: Date)
    unique by (period)
    append only

-- The schedule's chain: each period follows exactly one prior
-- period. Unique on the prior means no fork (a period has at most
-- one successor); unique on the next means no merge (a period has
-- at most one predecessor). Append only: the chain is never
-- quietly re-linked.
predicate Follows(next: Subject, prior: Subject)
    unique by (prior)
    unique by (next)
    append only

-- A delivered compliance certificate for one period, with the date
-- it arrived. One certificate per period, and delivery is an event
-- that happened - it does not unhappen.
predicate Certificate(facility: Subject, period: Subject, delivered_on: Date)
    unique by (period)
    append only

-- Standing, not storage: Timely says the record accepts the
-- period's certificate as having landed inside the window. It is
-- granted by an act below and defended by the invariants - a
-- certificate can exist without ever earning this.
predicate Timely(period: Subject)

-- An overdue notice: as of a stated date, the period's certificate
-- had not arrived, and it was days_late days past the deadline.
-- The count is the claim's whole point - default interest runs on
-- it - which is exactly why the caller does not get to choose it.
predicate Overdue(period: Subject, as_of: Date, days_late: Decimal)
    unique by (period)

-- ------------------------------------------------------------
-- The calendar rules
-- ------------------------------------------------------------

-- A schedule link may only connect two periods of the same
-- facility, and both must exist. Without this, a link across
-- facilities would quietly escape the rolling rule below.
invariant follows_links_periods_of_one_facility:
    Follows(next, prior) implies (exists f: TestPeriod(f, next, _) and TestPeriod(f, prior, _))

-- Each period ends three calendar months after the one it follows.
-- That is the agreement's own convention here: intervals from the
-- prior test date, anniversary style. Be careful what that means.
-- A calendar month is not a fixed length, so when the day does not
-- exist in the destination month, the shift lands on the month's
-- last day: 30 November + span(P3M) is 28 February (29 in a leap
-- year). And clamping makes the walk drift: two three-month hops
-- from 30 November reach 28 May, while one six-month shift reaches
-- 30 May. Neither is wrong - that is what the words "three months
-- after the previous date" do around month ends. An agreement that
-- wants fixed quarter-ends instead simply states those dates, and
-- this rule would pin them the same way.
invariant periods_follow_three_month_anniversaries:
    Follows(next, prior) and TestPeriod(f, next, next_ends) and TestPeriod(f, prior, prior_ends)
    implies next_ends = prior_ends + span(P3M)

-- ------------------------------------------------------------
-- The certificate window
-- ------------------------------------------------------------

-- A certificate names a period the record actually has.
invariant certificates_name_their_period:
    Certificate(f, p, _) implies TestPeriod(f, p, _)

-- Timely standing without a certificate would be an empty honour;
-- this is the companion that makes the window rule below total.
invariant timely_standing_names_a_certificate:
    Timely(p) implies (exists f: Certificate(f, p, _))

-- The window itself: standing holds only if delivery came on or
-- before the 45th day after the period end. The deadline is not a
-- stored date anyone maintains - it is computed from the period's
-- own end, each time, inside the rule.
invariant timely_certificates_landed_inside_the_window:
    Timely(p) and Certificate(f, p, delivered_on) and TestPeriod(f, p, ends_on)
    implies delivered_on on_or_before ends_on + span(P45D)

-- ------------------------------------------------------------
-- Overdue notices
-- ------------------------------------------------------------

-- A notice names a period the record has...
invariant overdue_notices_name_a_period:
    Overdue(p, _, _) implies (exists f: TestPeriod(f, p, _))

-- ...and only stands once the deadline has genuinely passed.
invariant overdue_notices_follow_the_deadline:
    Overdue(p, as_of, _) and TestPeriod(f, p, ends_on)
    implies (ends_on + span(P45D)) before as_of

-- The count on the notice is the record's own count: the days from
-- the deadline to the notice date, by the calendar. The caller
-- states a figure; if it differs by a single day, the notice does
-- not commit. Days here are actual days - the count a default
-- interest clause multiplies.
invariant overdue_notices_state_the_records_own_lateness:
    let deadline = (ends_on + span(P45D))
    Overdue(p, as_of, days_late) and TestPeriod(f, p, ends_on)
    implies days_late = as_of - deadline

-- A notice and a delivery must agree on the order of events: the
-- notice date comes strictly before any delivery date. Read it in
-- both directions. A notice cannot be declared for a period whose
-- certificate had already arrived - and once a notice stands, a
-- certificate backdated to before it is refused, whichever act is
-- proposed first.
invariant overdue_notices_precede_any_delivery:
    Overdue(p, as_of, _) and Certificate(f, p, delivered_on)
    implies as_of before delivered_on

-- ------------------------------------------------------------
-- The acts
-- ------------------------------------------------------------

-- Opening admits the facility and its first period in one act, so
-- the anchor and the first calendar entry cannot disagree - they
-- are the same value by construction.
transformation open_facility(facility, first_period, first_period_ends):
    admit Facility(facility, first_period_ends)
    admit TestPeriod(facility, first_period, first_period_ends)

-- Scheduling states the next period's end date; the anniversary
-- rule above refuses any date that is not exactly three calendar
-- months after the prior period's end.
transformation schedule_next_period(facility, prior, next, ends_on):
    require the_prior_period_exists: TestPeriod(facility, prior, _)
    admit TestPeriod(facility, next, ends_on)
    admit Follows(next, prior)

-- Delivery is recorded whenever it happens - late delivery is a
-- late delivery, not an inadmissible document. Whether it earns
-- Timely standing is a separate act.
transformation submit_certificate(facility, period, delivered_on):
    require the_period_exists: TestPeriod(facility, period, _)
    admit Certificate(facility, period, delivered_on)

-- Granting standing looks up the period and the certificate the
-- record already holds and gates on the window. The invariant
-- above re-states the same rule over the committed record, so the
-- standing stays honest even against later proposals.
transformation accept_timely(facility, period):
    bind TestPeriod(facility, period, ends_on)
    bind Certificate(facility, period, delivered_on)
    require delivery_landed_inside_the_window: delivered_on on_or_before ends_on + span(P45D)
    admit Timely(period)

-- Declaring a period overdue: the deadline must have passed, and
-- nothing may have been delivered by the notice date. The lateness
-- figure is the caller's statement; the exactness invariant is what
-- makes it trustworthy.
transformation declare_overdue(facility, period, as_of, days_late):
    bind TestPeriod(facility, period, ends_on)
    let deadline = ends_on + span(P45D)
    require the_deadline_has_passed: deadline before as_of
    require nothing_was_delivered_by_then: not (exists d: Certificate(facility, period, d) and d on_or_before as_of)
    admit Overdue(period, as_of, days_late)
